This paper explores an emerging sensing modality for wearable metabolic-rate estimation: using a harvesting-optimized thermoelectric generator (TEG) as a metabolic sensor. Rather than optimizing harvesting hardware or studying intermittent runtimes, we treat the electrical energy produced by a commercial wrist-worn TEG as a physiological signal reflecting on-body heat transfer. Because harvested energy is influenced by both metabolic heat and transport effects (e.g., convection, contact, and microclimate), we pair it with lightweight thermal and motion context to assess feasibility. We present CalTEG, a compact wrist platform that logs harvested energy, TEG interface temperatures, ambient temperature/humidity, and optional inertial/heart-rate inputs. We first benchmark CalTEG in free-living conditions, validating harvested-power-derived heat-flux estimates against an instrumentation-grade GreenTEG reference, and then relate TEG signals to ground-truth metabolism in an IRB-approved room calorimeter study with 16 participants. Across rolling multi-minute windows, we evaluate sensing configurations that trade accuracy for power, ranging from harvester-only to thermally instrumented and context-augmented variants. Harvested energy alone is informative but insufficient for reliable short-window estimation. Adding minimal thermal instrumentation and low-power motion sensing substantially improves accuracy, yielding up to 44% lower cumulative MAPE than a leading commercial fitness tracker and outperforming an apples-to-apples heart-rate baseline under the same modeling pipeline. Finally, we identify vigorous activity as a transport-dominated regime where wrist heat transfer can temporarily decouple from whole-body metabolism, outlining this as the key frontier for robust free-living commercial-TEG metabolic sensing.